Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtlwifi: rtw89: allow bypassing the chip rfkill poll

The RTL8852CE on the RK3588 TSPI-4M reads its kill state from its own
internal GPIO9 and never reports it as released:

  rtw89_8852ce 0002:21:00.0: rfkill hardware state changed to disable

That lands in wiphy_rfkill_set_hw_state(), so mac80211 blocks the wiphy
for the whole boot. Writing /sys/class/rfkill/rfkill1/state cannot help.
The driver re-polls the chip on every poll work and pushes it back to
blocked.

Several boards wire no kill line, or wire it active permanently. A stuck
input then blocks the radio on a board with no kill switch at all.

Add a device tree boolean, opt-in per board:

  rtw89,bypass-hw-rfkill;

Put it on the PCI host bridge node, for example pcie2x1l0. A PCIe device
only has its own of_node when the host bridge carries a PCI device node
for it, and these boards do not. Walk the device ancestry to find the
property on the platform host controller. PCI root ports and the host
bridge can have no of_node, so checking one parent misses the property.

Software rfkill keeps working through wiphy_rfkill_block(), so userspace
still controls the radio.

Signed-off-by: Armbian <info@armbian.com>
---

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/core.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/core.c
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@
 /* Copyright(c) 2019-2020  Realtek Corporation
  */
 #include <linux/ip.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/sort.h>
 #include <linux/udp.h>
 
@@ -6138,6 +6139,14 @@
 
 static bool rtw89_chip_has_rfkill(struct rtw89_dev *rtwdev)
 {
+	struct device *dev;
+
+	for (dev = rtwdev->dev; dev; dev = dev->parent) {
+		if (of_property_read_bool(dev->of_node,
+					  "rtw89,bypass-hw-rfkill"))
+			return false;
+	}
+
 	return !!rtwdev->chip->rfkill_init;
 }
